# /srv/fdroid/.ssh/authorized_keys  (chown fdroid:fdroid, chmod 600)
#
# The restriction is the point. A bare public key here grants a full shell as
# the fdroid user; with this prefix the key can do exactly one thing, rsync
# under /srv/www, with no shell, no port forwarding and no agent.
#
# The root is /srv/fdroid/web, a subdirectory, and NOT /srv/fdroid itself --
# that is the fdroid user's home. rrsync has no protection for dotfiles (it
# blocks only ".." and pins the root), so rooted at the home this key could
# overwrite ~/.ssh/authorized_keys, or write ~/.bashrc, which sshd's forced
# command executes because bash reads it when run non-interactively over a
# network connection. Either one turns rsync access into a shell.
#
# Read and write, not rrsync -wo: the publish job pulls the repo down before
# regenerating the index, and fdroid deploy rsyncs with --delete-after.
command="rrsync /srv/fdroid/web",restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIJJYHbb61gUXRVcrcLWvAkbO5aLJn16ZC3lhlhm7bsyy dev@desktop
